.\" conn(1) is written by hand. The release archive carries it beside
.\" the binary, and install.sh puts it where man finds it. The release
.\" stamps the .TH line with its version.
.TH CONN 1 "2026-10-16" "conn 0.24.0" "User Commands"
.
.SH NAME
conn \- a console for the processes working your projects
.
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B conn
.RI [ \-\-light | \-\-dark ]
.RI [ \-\-theme
.IR name ]
.br
.B conn ls
.br
.B conn l
.I address
.RB [ \-n
.IR lines ]
.br
.B conn u
.I name
.RB [ \-w ]
.B \-\-
.IR command ...
.br
.B conn x
.I address
.br
.B conn r
.I address
.br
.B conn theme claude
.
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B conn
shows the processes working a project on this machine, puts the one you
choose in front of you, and works them by a few keys that agents reach
by the same names from a shell.
It reads what the machine already records: the git checkouts under your
home, the panes of its own tmux server, the process table and the
sockets through
.BR ps (1)
and
.BR lsof (8),
and the files Claude Code keeps of its sessions.
It is told nothing: no roots, no declarations.
What a project runs it learns from what is run in it through
.BR conn ,
and asks Claude Code only where nobody has run anything yet.
.PP
The first
.B conn
brings up a tmux server of its own, on its own socket, with one window.
.B conn
is the
.I panel
on the left of that window, 50 columns wide.
The rest of the window is the
.IR workspace ,
which shows the process under the cursor;
.B enter
puts the keys in it.
Every process
.B conn
starts runs in a window of the server out of sight, and is swapped into
the workspace to be shown.
A later
.B conn
attaches to the server that is up.
A
.B conn
of another build starts the panel again on itself and leaves every other
process running: an update is had by running the new
.BR conn .
.B q
detaches; the server and every process keep running.
Nothing takes the server down but
.B x
on each project, or tmux's own
.B kill\-server
on
.BR conn 's
socket.
.PP
One key,
.BR ctrl\-space ,
brings the keys back to the panel from inside any process.
It is the only key
.B conn
binds; tmux's prefix,
.BR ctrl\-b ,
is left as tmux has it.
.PP
Without
.BR tmux (1)
on the PATH,
.B conn
says so and does nothing.
.B conn panel
and
.B conn hold
are run by
.B conn
in panes of its server.
They are not for typing.
.
.SH THE SCREENS
.SS The projects
The first screen, and
.B \-
from a project.
Every project under your home, a row a project: its name, its path, and
at the right what waits and what runs in it,
.BR "1 waiting \(pc 2 running" ,
or how long ago work last happened there.
A project where a conversation waits is first, in the accent; the rest
newest work first.
A folder of repositories is a row of its own,
.IR name /,
with its repositories under it, and opens as a project of all of
theirs.
A line to type into narrows the rows: each word typed, in the name or
the path.
A path typed,
.I ~/x
or anything with a slash in it, names a directory, and
.B enter
opens it as a project whether or not it was found: the repository it is
in where it is in one, the directory itself otherwise.
.SS A project
The project's name at the head, its path after it, and its rows: what
runs in it, by role, in order: conversations, the stack, what talks to
it, the checks, what it talks to outside
.BR conn ,
and conversations running in terminals
.B conn
does not hold.
See
.B WHAT A ROW SAYS
below.
Under the rows, under a label
.B projects
with a rule, a line for every other project with something running,
waiting or at work in it; the cursor runs over rows and lines alike,
and
.B enter
on a line opens that project.
A project with nothing running says so, and the keys that start
something.
.PP
Rows that run in a worktree of the project stand together under a head
of their own, the branch the worktree has out, a step in; in a folder,
each row says its repository before its name.
.SS The keys
\fB?\fR lays the screen's keys over it, a key a line, in full.
The bar across the foot of the window has only the commonest, a word
each.
Any key puts them away.
.SS The search
.B /
in a project: a line to type into, and under it every line the
project's panes still hold that says what is typed, newest first, under
the name of the row each pane is, the text in bold; at the right how
many lines.
Case is ignored while nothing typed is a capital, as tmux and vim take
a search.
The lines are as they were when
.B /
was pressed.
The workspace shows the line under the cursor in its pane, in tmux's
copy mode on the match with every saying lit, the keys staying on the
panel;
.B enter
puts the keys there, where
.B n
and
.B N
go on to the next saying and
.B q
leaves, as in any pane.
.B esc
puts the search away.
A row with no pane, a listener outside
.BR conn ,
has nothing here.
.SS A question
.BR ss ,
.B st
and
.B c
on a process nobody has run or talked to here ask Claude Code, and the
screen is the question while it stands: asking, then what Claude Code
answered, a name and a command a line, under
.BR "Start these in \fIproject\fB?" ,
.B "Run these checks in \fIproject\fB?"
or
.BR "Talk to it with this?" .
.B enter
takes the answer and writes it down as what the role means here;
.B esc
lets it go.
What Claude Code would not say is said in its place.
See
.B WHAT A PROJECT RUNS
below.
.SS The conversations at rest
.B rc
in a project: a line to type into, and under it the project's
conversations that no running Claude Code is carrying, newest first,
the fifty most recent, each by its title and how long ago it moved.
.B enter
picks the one under the cursor back up in a window of its own, at the
directory it was had in;
.B esc
puts the list away.
.SS The line
\fB!\fR in a project: a command typed on the foot of the panel,
.BR "run in \fIproject\fB: " .
.B enter
runs it where the cursor stands, out of sight, under the name of its
program, as
.B conn u
does, and nothing is written down of it; with nothing typed,
.B enter
opens a shell there and puts the keys in it.
.SS The settings
.B ,
on either screen: the themes
.B conn
has under one label and the two grounds under another, with
.B on
beside what the server is on.
.B enter
puts the server on the row's where it stands, and keeps it for the
next server.
See
.B THEMES
below.
.SS The hold
Where the cursor is on nothing with a pane, the workspace holds
.BR conn 's
blank,
.BR "nothing selected" .
A key in it puts the keys back on the panel.
.
.SH KEYS
What a key does depends on where the keys are.
The key bar across the foot of the window says the keys that work where
the cursor is, a word each; \fB?\fR says them in full.
.SS In a process
.TP 22
.B ctrl\-space
To the panel.
The process's project goes on the panel and its row under the cursor,
so the next key is about it.
.TP
.B ctrl\-space ctrl\-space
To the process you were in before this one.
Pressed again, back.
.TP
.B ctrl\-space tab
To the conversation that has waited longest for you.
.TP
.B ctrl\-space esc
Back in.
.PP
Everything else is the process's own, and tmux's: the wheel scrolls,
a drag copies, and
.B ctrl\-b
is tmux's prefix.
.SS On the projects screen
.TP 22
.I a character
Types, and the rows narrow to what the line says.
.TP
.BR up ", " down ", " ctrl\-p ", " ctrl\-n ", " ctrl\-k ", " ctrl\-j
Move the cursor a row.
The rows are a ring: past the last is the first.
.TP
.B enter
Open the project under the cursor; with none shown and a path typed,
the directory the path names.
.TP
.B tab
To the conversation that has waited longest for you, wherever it runs
in
.BR conn .
.TP
.B x
With nothing typed: stop everything in the project under the cursor.
Every process
.B conn
holds in it, conversations included, which keep their transcripts, and
every server in it outside
.BR conn ;
not a server the project only connects to.
.TP
.B esc
Clear what was typed; with nothing typed, back down into the project
you came up from.
.TP
.B ctrl\-u
Clear what was typed.
.TP
.B ,
With nothing typed: the settings.
.TP
.B ?
The keys.
.TP
.B ctrl\-c
Detach.
Everything in
.B conn
keeps running.
.SS In a project
.TP 22
.BR j ", " k ", " down ", " up ", " ctrl\-n ", " ctrl\-p
Move the cursor a row.
The rows and the other projects' lines are one ring.
.TP
.BR g ", " G
The first row, the last.
.TP
.B enter
On a row with a pane: put the pane in the workspace and the keys in it.
On a conversation in a terminal
.B conn
does not hold: say so.
On another project's line: open that project.
.TP
.B tab
To the conversation that has waited longest for you.
Again: the next one, round.
.TP
.B esc
Back into the process the keys were last in, its project on the
panel.
.TP
.B ctrl\-space
To the process you were in before that one.
.TP
.B \-
Up to the projects.
.TP
.B c
Talk to the row's process; see
.B c
below.
.TP
.B !
The line: a command to run here, or a shell.
.TP
.B /
The search.
.TP
.B ,
The settings.
.TP
.B ?
The keys.
.TP
.BR q ", " ctrl\-c
Detach.
.PP
The other letters are verbs, and a verb waits for its object, as an
operator waits in
.BR vim (1):
the bar says what it waits for, and
.B esc
or any key that is not an object lets it go, the foot saying what it
waited for.
The verb again is the row under the cursor, as
.B dd
is the line.
.TP 22
.B s
Start.
.B ss
brings up the stack and
.B st
runs the checks, as each was last run here by whoever ran it, and asks
Claude Code where nobody has yet; see
.BR "WHAT A PROJECT RUNS" .
.B sc
starts a conversation.
.TP
.B x
Stop, with no question asked.
.B xx
stops the row's process: what
.B conn
started under a name is sent
.B ctrl\-c
and its pane closed once it has ended, or after five seconds anyway;
any other pane is closed, a conversation's included, which keeps its
transcript; a server outside
.B conn
is sent
.BR SIGTERM .
.B xx
on another project's line stops everything in it, as
.B x
on the projects screen does.
.B xs
stops the stack and
.B xt
the checks, as they stand on the panel;
.B xp
everything in the open project.
.TP
.B r
Restart.
.B rr
starts the row's process again as it was started: a named process is
sent
.B ctrl\-c
and run again in its pane; a conversation is picked back up in its
pane; any other pane runs again what it was opened with; a server
outside
.B conn
was started nobody knows how, and is not.
.B rs
restarts the stack and
.B rt
the checks as they stand, or runs them as
.B ss
and
.B st
do where none stand;
.B rf
restarts every run that failed.
.B rc
lists the conversations at rest.
.TP
.B a
Hand to an agent.
.B aa
starts a conversation in the project whose first prompt is the row's
process: its name, what it runs and where, how it is doing, and the
last lines it wrote, and what to do about it.
.B af
hands every run that failed to one conversation, each with its last
lines in a share of the size.
A conversation is an agent already and is not handed.
.TP
.B c
Talk to it.
A conversation or a shell is gone into.
A process with a client kept under its name here opens it; see
.BR "WHAT A PROJECT RUNS" .
Otherwise the lowest of its ports that answers HTTP opens in the
browser, at
.IR localhost: port ,
and is kept as its client.
Otherwise Claude Code is asked what opens a session with it, and the
one line it answers is offered;
.B enter
opens it and keeps it, a URL in the browser or a command in a window of
its own at the project with the keys in it.
The browser is the system's own:
.BR open (1)
on macOS,
.BR xdg\-open (1)
on Linux.
.PP
A verb starts things where the cursor stands: in the checkout of the
row under it, a worktree's or the project's, and a folder's repository
in that checkout where it is the repository's, at the repository
otherwise.
.SS On the line
.TP 22
.I a character
Types.
.TP
.B enter
Run the line; with nothing typed, open a shell.
.TP
.B ctrl\-u
Clear the line.
.TP
.BR esc ", " ctrl\-c
Put the line away.
.SS In the search
.TP 22
.I a character
Types, and the lines narrow to what is typed.
.TP
.BR up ", " down ", " ctrl\-p ", " ctrl\-n ", " ctrl\-k ", " ctrl\-j
Move among the lines found; the workspace follows.
.TP
.B enter
Into the line's pane, in copy mode on the match, the keys there.
.TP
.B ctrl\-u
Clear what is typed.
.TP
.BR esc ", " ctrl\-c
Put the search away.
.SS In the conversations at rest
.TP 22
.I a character
Types, and the conversations narrow to those whose title says every
word.
.TP
.BR up ", " down ", " ctrl\-p ", " ctrl\-n ", " ctrl\-k ", " ctrl\-j
Move the cursor.
.TP
.B enter
Pick the conversation back up, in a window of its own, the keys in it.
.TP
.BR esc ", " ctrl\-c
Put the list away.
.SS With a question standing
.TP 22
.B enter
Take the answer: start each process named, or open the client, and
write it down.
.TP
.BR esc ", " q ", " ctrl\-c
Let it go.
While Claude Code is still asked,
.B esc
stops waiting; nothing started is undone.
.SS In the settings
.TP 22
.BR j ", " k ", " up ", " down ", " ctrl\-n ", " ctrl\-p
Move the cursor a row, around at the ends.
.TP
.BR g ", " G
The first row, the last.
.TP
.BR enter ", " space
Put the server on the row's theme or ground.
.TP
.B esc
Close the settings; so do \fB,\fR again, \fBq\fR and \fBctrl\-c\fR.
.SS In the keys
.TP 22
.B any key
Puts them away, and does nothing else.
.
.SH WHAT A ROW SAYS
.SS Marks
A row's mark is its role, in a cell after the margin, so the name is
only the name.
.TP 6
.B ❝
A conversation: someone speaking at the other end.
.TP
.B ○
The stack: a process started under a name through
.B conn
that keeps running.
.TP
.B ❯
What talks to the stack: a shell, or a client
.B c
opened.
The prompt a shell shows you.
.TP
.B ◦
A check: a process started under a name through
.B conn
that runs to completion.
The small ring while it runs;
.B ✓
in the running green once it passed;
.B ✗
in red once it failed.
.TP
.B ◌
A listener outside
.BR conn ,
the ring unfilled, held by nobody.
.PP
The mark takes the name's ink; a verdict keeps its own under the
cursor.
.SS Places
A project's rows stand by role, in order: conversations, the stack,
what talks to it, the checks, what it talks to outside
.BR conn ,
and conversations running in terminals
.B conn
does not hold.
A row stands in its place whatever its state: wanting you is a color,
not a move.
In a place, what the project has written down stands in the order the
names were first written, and the rest in the order it was started.
A named process is one row across its runs: run again, it keeps its
row and the cursor.
.SS The margin
Three columns before the mark.
The row under the cursor carries a bar in the first.
In the second a spinner turns beside a row at work on something that
will come to rest, a conversation working or a check running, and a
full cell stands beside a process running persistently, the stack's
and a listener outside
.BR conn .
.SS The name
A conversation's is its title: the last rename or title Claude Code
wrote into its transcript, else the last prompt, else its id; one just
started with nothing said yet is
.BR "new conversation" .
A named process is the name it was started under; a shell or a client
is what tmux says runs in it; a listener outside
.B conn
is its program's name.
A process started below the checkout the row stands in says its
directory after the name.
.SS The figure at the right
The one thing worth knowing of the row without going in.
A conversation running says the context its last turn ran in,
.BR 262k ,
and
.B waiting
in the accent where it waits on you; one in another terminal says so.
A process running says its ports,
.BR ":8123 :8124" .
A run that ended says how long ago; one of the stack that failed says
its status first,
.BR "failed 1" ,
in red; a check's mark says its verdict, so its figure is the age
alone.
A row up with nothing to report says nothing.
.SS Inks
What runs is in the ink, what waits on you in the accent, a run that
failed in the terminal's red, a conversation at rest in the gray.
The row under the cursor is on the chosen ground from edge to edge.
.SS The other projects' lines
Under the label
.BR projects ,
a line a project with something running, waiting or at work in it: its
name, in the accent where a conversation waits, the spinner in the
margin where something is at work, and at the right what waits and
what runs.
A folder of repositories has no line of its own, nor has a repository
of the open folder.
.SS The foot
Under the rows, a line of
.BR conn 's
own until the next key: what the last key did or could not do, in its
words, as
.BR "web is up" ,
.BR "web ended: send\-keys C\-c" ,
.B "the stack: ended 2"
or
.BR "Nothing is waiting on you." ;
a verb let go says what it waited for.
.
.SH PROJECTS
A project is a git repository, known by git's common directory, so a
worktree's work is its repository's wherever the worktree is, and a
bare repository's worktrees are the repository's.
A directory in no repository is a project of its own while
.B conn
holds a pane in it.
Home is never a project, nor is a directory the system clears,
.I /tmp
and its kin.
.PP
The projects are the checkouts under your home, four directories deep,
looked for again every thirty seconds, passing over hidden directories,
.IR Library ,
.I node_modules
and
.IR vendor ;
and whatever else
.B conn
holds a pane in.
When work last happened in one is read from Claude Code's transcripts,
each by the directory it was had in, and from
.BR conn 's
panes.
.PP
A directory not directly in your home that directly holds two or more
checkouts is a folder of repositories: listed with them beneath it,
and a project of all of theirs, in which each row says its repository.
Work done in the folder itself is the folder's.
.PP
A project's rows are the conversations had in it, the panes of
.BR conn 's
server whose directory is in it, and the listeners outside
.B conn
whose directory is in it or that those panes hold a connection to on
this machine: a database brew keeps under launchd is a row of every
project whose dev server talks to it.
.
.SH WHAT A PROJECT RUNS
.B conn
is not told what a project runs.
It writes down every process started under a name through it, by an
agent at the command line or from the keys, with its role: a
.IR check ,
which
.B conn u \-w
waited on and which runs to completion and passes or fails, or the
.IR stack ,
what keeps running while the project is worked on.
The file is one line a start, beside the socket; the last line for a
name in a project is what the name means there, and a name run since as
the other role has changed its meaning.
.PP
.B ss
brings up the stack and
.B st
runs the checks, each name as it was last run here and in the order the
names were first run, in the checkout the cursor stands in.
A name that is running already is left, and said; one whose pane holds
its last run is started again in its place.
.PP
Where nobody has run a role here, Claude Code is asked, in print mode
with no tools, as
.BR "claude \-p" ,
and given ninety seconds: the project's path, the names at its top, and
the first eighty lines of each of
.IR Procfile ,
.IR Procfile.dev ,
.IR package.json ,
.IR compose.yaml ,
.IR compose.yml ,
.IR docker\-compose.yml ,
.IR Makefile ,
.IR justfile ,
.IR Cargo.toml ,
.IR go.mod ,
.IR pyproject.toml ,
.I Gemfile
and
.IR README.md ,
and asked for the long-running processes a developer starts, or the
checks a developer runs before calling work done, one a line as
.IR name : " command" .
The answer is offered on the screen;
.B enter
starts each in a window of its own under its name and writes it down as
the role here; the next
.B ss
runs what was written, and asks nothing.
.PP
What
.B ss
and
.B st
start is watched for two seconds.
A run that ends in an error by then, as written down or as Claude Code
just named it, is more often a command that does not work here than a
project that is broken: Claude Code is asked again, given each such run,
its status and its last lines.
A command that works is offered in place of the old, under the same
name,
.BR "Start these instead?" ,
and
.B enter
writes it down; a command that is right and failed for the project's
own reasons, a test that fails, is said to be the project's, and nothing
is offered.
.PP
.B c
on a process that is neither a conversation nor a shell keeps what talks
to it under the process's name, as a
.IR client ,
without taking the name from it: the URL the browser opened, or the one
line Claude Code answered, asked the same way and told the process by
its name, its command and its ports.
.PP
Every conversation
.B conn
starts or picks back up is told what the project's checks and stack are
here, as
.B conn u
lines; see
.BR CONVERSATIONS .
.
.SH CONVERSATIONS
A conversation is a session of Claude Code, the one agent
.B conn
reads the state of.
Claude Code records each running instance in a file under its
configuration directory;
.B conn
reads it, trusts it only if the process is alive and began when the
file says, and takes its word:
.B busy
and
.B shell
are working,
.B waiting
is waiting on you,
.B idle
is at rest between turns.
A running conversation is matched to a pane of
.BR conn 's
server by the mark
.B conn
set when it started it, or by the terminal it runs on; one running in a
terminal
.B conn
does not hold is a row all the same, saying so, which
.B enter
cannot go into.
A transcript no running instance is carrying is a conversation at rest,
in
.BR rc 's
list.
.PP
.B sc
starts Claude Code in a window of its own at the checkout the cursor
stands in, under a session id
.B conn
chose, so the row is a conversation before the transcript exists.
.B aa
and
.B af
start one the same way with a first prompt.
.B rc
and
.B rr
pick one back up with
.BR "claude \-\-resume" ,
at the directory it was had in, or the project's root where that is
gone.
.PP
Every conversation
.B conn
starts or picks back up has a note appended to its system prompt: that
it runs inside
.BR conn ,
and how to work the project's processes through it rather than in the
background or through
.BR ps ,
.B lsof
and
.BR tmux :
.BR "conn ls" ,
.BR "conn u" ,
with
.B \-w
for a check,
.BR "conn l" ,
.B "conn r"
and
.BR "conn x" ;
that what it starts this way is a pane the operator can go into and
read, and is kept as what the project runs; and the project's checks and
stack as last run here, as
.B conn u
lines.
Where the
.B conn
the PATH finds is not the build that started it, the note names the
build by its whole path.
The note is not shown on the row.
.PP
A conversation's title is read from the tail of its transcript, and the
context it carries from the last turn's usage.
.B tab
goes to the conversation that has waited longest, the next one each
press, round.
The number waiting, over every project, is on the status line and in
the terminal's title, and the terminal's bell rings as one more starts
waiting.
.
.SH THE STATUS LINE
Two rows across the foot of the window, drawn by tmux from what
.B conn
tells it.
.SS The band
At the left, the wordmark.
At the right, in the accent, how many conversations wait on you,
.BR "2 waiting" ,
and nothing while none does.
The terminal's title says the same:
.BR conn ,
or
.BR "conn \(pc 2 waiting" .
.SS The key bar
While the keys are on the panel, the keys that work where the cursor is,
a word each, on the accent's block, so being in
.B conn
shows from anywhere on the screen.
While they are in a pane,
.BR "ctrl\-space back to conn" .
While a pane is in copy mode, copy mode's own keys:
.BR "n N" ,
.BR "/ ?" ,
.BR "v y" ,
.BR q .
.
.SH COMMANDS
The command line is
.B conn
for whoever has no panel: an agent, a script, a person at a shell.
It works the processes of the project the working directory is in, and
knows a process the way an agent does: by the name it was started
under, by the port an error named, or by the pid
.B conn ls
gave.
.TP 18
.B conn
The projects screen, then a project.
In the server: attach.
On a server running another build of
.BR conn ,
the panel is started again on this one and the processes in the server
are left running.
Typed in a pane of the server, it puts the keys on
.BR conn 's
window; with a flag, it puts the server on the ground or theme the flag
names where it stands.
On a server an earlier
.B conn
arranged, it asks whether to end it and everything in it.
.TP
.B conn ls
The processes of the project the working directory is in, read once.
The first line is the project's path; then a line a process: the name
it answers to, its pid, what it is doing, the ports it listens on or
.BR \- ,
the command, and in parentheses its directory where that is not the
project's.
The processes are
.BR conn 's
panes in the project, and the listeners outside
.B conn
whose directory is in the project or that those panes connect to.
What a process is doing is
.BR running ,
.BR working ,
.BR waiting ,
.B idle
for a conversation,
.B done
or
.BI "failed " n
for a run that ended, and
.B "outside conn"
for a listener.
With no server up, the listeners alone.
.TP
.BI "conn l " address " [\-n " lines ]
What a process said: the lines its pane holds, history and screen,
wrapped lines joined.
The last 100;
.B \-n
says how many, and
.B "\-n 0"
all of them.
A listener outside
.B conn
has no pane, and
.B conn l
says so.
.IP
An address is a name, as
.B conn ls
gives it,
.BI : port
for the process listening on a port, or a pid.
A port or a pid names one process on the machine, in any project.
A name is looked for in the project; where two answer to it, the one in
the caller's own checkout, and then the one still running, is meant;
two still is an error that gives their pids.
.TP
.BI "conn u " name " [\-w] \-\- " command ...
Start a command in a window of
.BR conn 's
server at the working directory, under a name: a row on the panel,
which
.B conn l
reads and
.B conn x
ends by the name.
The name is one word of letters, digits, dot, underscore and hyphen,
and not a number.
One word after
.B \-\-
is a line for
.BR sh (1)
as written, pipes and all; several are a command and its arguments,
each quoted where
.B sh
would read it otherwise.
A name that is up already is an error; one whose pane holds its last
run is started again in place of it.
The start is written down as what the project runs: a check with
.BR \-w ,
the stack without; see
.BR "WHAT A PROJECT RUNS" .
Prints the pane it is up in.
.IP
Run from a pane of
.BR conn 's,
the start is that pane's project's wherever the working directory is:
an agent that goes into another checkout to run its project's site
tests there runs a check of its own project, on the panel and in what
is written down, and the row says where it ran.
.IP
.B \-w
waits for the command to end, prints what it said, and exits with its
status: a check run this way is on the panel while it runs, and its end
is the caller's answer.
Ended from outside before it recorded a status, it is an error.
.IP
Needs the server up.
.TP
.BI "conn x " address
Stop a process, as
.B xx
does: what
.B conn u
started is sent
.B ctrl\-c
and its pane closed once it has ended, or after five seconds anyway;
any other pane is closed, a conversation's included; a listener outside
.B conn
is sent
.BR SIGTERM .
Prints what was done.
.IP
.B conn x
and
.B conn r
reach only the caller's project, the pane's from a pane of
.BR conn 's,
else the working directory's: a port or a pid of another project's
process is an error.
.TP
.BI "conn r " address
Start a process again, as
.B rr
does: what
.B conn u
started is sent
.B ctrl\-c
and run again in its pane, as it was started; a conversation is picked
back up in its pane; any other pane runs again what it was opened
with.
A listener outside
.B conn
was started nobody knows how, and is an error.
Prints the pane.
.TP
.B conn theme claude
Write
.BR conn 's
theme for Claude Code to Claude Code's themes directory as
.BR custom:conn ,
on the ground the server is on, or the ground a fresh server would come
up on.
If Claude Code is on one of its own themes,
.B conn
offers to switch it; if it is on a custom theme,
.B conn
says so and leaves it.
Once written, the file is rewritten whenever a server comes up or is
put on another ground.
.PP
Anything else after
.B conn
is an error that names the commands.
There is no
.B \-\-help
and no
.BR \-\-version .
.
.SH OPTIONS
Given before a command.
.TP 14
.B \-\-light
The ground is light.
.TP
.B \-\-dark
The ground is dark.
.TP
.BI \-\-theme " name"
The theme; also
.BI \-\-theme= name .
.PP
For a server coming up, these decide the ground and the theme, over
what the configuration file says.
For a server already up, they change it where it stands: every pane is
dressed again, and
.B \-\-theme
reads its sheet again even where the server wears it, so a sheet being
worked on is seen.
Without a flag, a fresh server takes
.B theme
and
.B ground
from the configuration file, and with no ground there asks the terminal
its background with OSC 11, calling it dark unless it answers light.
.B \-\-light
with
.B \-\-dark
is a contradiction.
.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B CONN_SOCKET
The server's socket.
Default: under the state directory.
.TP
.B XDG_CONFIG_HOME
Where the configuration directory is.
Default
.IR ~/.config .
.TP
.B XDG_STATE_HOME
Where the state directory is.
Default
.IR ~/.local/state .
.TP
.B CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR
Where Claude Code keeps its files.
Default
.IR ~/.claude .
.TP
.B PATH
.B conn
runs
.BR tmux ,
.BR git ,
.BR ps ,
.BR lsof ,
.B claude
and the browser's opener from it.
Without
.BR lsof ,
no row says its ports, and no listener outside
.B conn
is a row.
.PP
In the server,
.B conn
sets
.BR CONN=1 ,
.B COLORTERM=truecolor
and
.BR CLAUDE_CODE_TMUX_TRUECOLOR=1 .
.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/conn/config.json
The configuration.
Two keys, each of which may be left out:
.PP
.nf
.RS
{
  "theme": "conn",
  "ground": "dark"
}
.RE
.fi
.IP
Read for a server coming up; written by the settings, every other key
kept.
No
.B ground
is the terminal's own, asked when a server rises.
A ground that is neither dark nor light is an error.
.TP
.I ~/.config/conn/themes/
Theme sheets,
.IR NAME .json
each; see
.BR THEMES .
.TP
.I ~/.local/state/conn/
The state directory:
.I tmux.sock
the server's socket;
.I tmux.conf
what
.B conn
tells tmux, written on each start;
.I mode
the ground and theme the server came up on, one line,
.BR "dark conn" ,
forgotten when the server goes down;
.I started.jsonl
what was started under a name through
.BR conn ,
a line a start, with the project's root, the name, the command, the
role and when; it is kept beside the socket, so a server on a socket of
its own keeps its own.
.TP
.I ~/.claude/sessions/
Claude Code's record of each running instance, read for a
conversation's state.
.TP
.I ~/.claude/projects/
Claude Code's transcripts, read for where work has happened, a
conversation's title and context, and the conversations at rest.
.TP
.I ~/.claude/themes/conn.json
Written by
.BR "conn theme claude" .
.TP
.I ~/.claude/settings.json
Read by
.B conn theme claude
for the theme in use; its
.B theme
key rewritten only with your consent.
.
.SH THEMES
.B conn
ships three themes,
.BR conn ,
.B datum
and
.BR skelly ,
each with a dark and a light ground, and wears any sheet in its themes
directory by the sheet's name; a sheet there under a shipped name stands
in for it.
The theme is
.B theme
in the configuration file,
.BR \-\-theme ,
or the settings; the ground is
.B ground
in the file,
.BR \-\-light / \-\-dark ,
or the settings; with none of those it is asked of the terminal when a
server rises.
Picking either in the settings dresses the server where it stands.
.PP
A sheet is a JSON object with a
.B dark
ground, a
.B light
one, or both; with one, it is worn on either.
A ground is its
.BR ground ,
its
.BR ink ,
the sixteen of its
.B scheme
that a program asks for by name, and every role by name, each a color
written
.BR #RRGGBB :
.BR accent ,
.BR shimmer ,
.BR atWork ,
.BR atWorkShimmer ,
.BR border ,
.BR surface ,
.BR running ,
.BR turning ,
.BR gray ,
.BR faint ,
.BR parchment ,
.BR block ,
.BR onBlock ,
.BR chosen ,
.BR chosenInk ,
.BR chosenMark ,
.BR cursor ,
.BR messageBg ,
.BR messageHoverBg ,
.BR toolBg ,
.BR diffAddedBg ,
.BR diffRemovedBg ,
.BR diffAddedDim ,
.BR diffRemovedDim ,
.BR diffAddedWord ,
.BR diffRemovedWord .
Anything missing, misspelt or not a color is said, by name.
Every color
.B conn
draws is by its role, and the sixteen ANSI colors of every pane in the
server are set to the theme's.
.
.SH TMUX
.B conn
runs its own tmux server on its own socket.
A tmux of yours on the default socket is not touched.
In
.BR conn 's
server:
.TP 4
\(bu
The prefix is tmux's own,
.BR ctrl\-b .
One key is bound in the root table, the panel key.
.TP
\(bu
The mouse is on: the wheel scrolls a pane, a drag copies, a click puts
the keys in a pane. A click on a row of the panel puts the cursor on it
and goes in, as enter does; on another project's line, or on a project
of the projects screen, it opens the project.
.TP
\(bu
Copy mode uses vi keys, and the system clipboard takes what is copied.
.TP
\(bu
Each pane keeps 10000 lines of history.
.TP
\(bu
The status line is
.BR conn 's,
two rows at the foot.
Windows have no tabs; the panel lists what runs.
.TP
\(bu
A window is named for its project, or for the name a process was
started under.
.TP
\(bu
The terminal's title is set, and says how many conversations wait.
.PP
A tmux command typed by hand reaches the server with
.BI "tmux \-S " socket ;
the socket is under
.BR FILES .
.B kill\-server
there takes it down with everything in it.
.
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
.TP
.B conn: conn knows ls, l, u, x, r and theme; with nothing after it, it opens on your projects
Exit 1.
.TP
.B conn: conn runs in tmux, and there is no tmux on the PATH
Exit 1.
.TP
.B conn: \-\-dark and \-\-light are a contradiction
Exit 1.
.TP
.B conn: \-\-theme names a theme
Exit 1.
.TP
.BI "conn: there is no theme " name "; there are " names ", in " dir
Exit 1.
.TP
.BI "conn: " path ": the ground is dark or light, not " word
Exit 1.
.TP
.B An earlier conn's server is up. End it, and everything running in it? [y/N]
Asked on the terminal.
Anything but yes leaves it, with
.BR "conn: left the earlier conn running" .
.TP
.B conn: conn's own window is gone; leave with ctrl\-b d and run conn again
Typed in a pane of the server.
Exit 1.
.TP
.B usage: conn ls
.TQ
.B usage: conn l NAME|:PORT|PID [\-n N]
.TQ
.B usage: conn u NAME [\-w] \-\- COMMAND...
.TQ
.B usage: conn x NAME|:PORT|PID
.TQ
.B usage: conn r NAME|:PORT|PID
Exit 2.
.TP
.BI "conn l: no process " name " in " project "; conn ls lists them"
And the same of
.BR x
and
.BR r ;
a port or a pid,
.BR "that conn knows" .
Exit 1.
.TP
.BI "conn l: " name " names " n " processes, pids " pids "; name one by its pid"
Exit 1.
.TP
.BI "conn l: " name " runs outside conn, so what it said went wherever whatever started it sent it"
Exit 1.
.TP
.B conn u: conn's server is not up; run conn first
Exit 1.
.TP
.BI "conn u: " name " is up, pid " pid "; conn r " name " starts it again"
Exit 1.
.TP
.B conn u: its pane was closed before it said how it ended
From
.BR \-w .
Exit 1.
.TP
.BI "conn x: " address " runs in " there ", not in " here "; conn x reaches only the project it is run in"
And the same of
.BR "conn r" .
Exit 1.
.TP
.BI "conn r: " name " was started outside conn, and conn does not know how; x ends it, and u starts a command in conn"
Exit 1.
.TP
.BI "conn r: " name " does not say what it was started with"
Exit 1.
.TP
.B conn: conn theme claude writes conn's theme for Claude Code
From
.B conn theme
with anything else.
Exit 1.
.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 on success; 1 when a command failed; 2 on a usage error.
.B conn u \-w
exits with the status of what it ran.
.
.SH CAVEATS
.TP 4
\(bu
Only Claude Code's state is read.
Another agent in a pane is a process like any other.
.TP
\(bu
A conversation running in a terminal
.B conn
does not hold is a row, and cannot be gone into.
.TP
\(bu
The ports are TCP listeners, from
.BR lsof .
.TP
\(bu
A listener outside
.B conn
is a row only while its directory is in the project or a pane of the
project connects to it.
.TP
\(bu
Asking Claude Code takes seconds, needs
.B claude
on the PATH, and is answered by a small model with no tools.
.TP
\(bu
What
.B !
runs is not written down; what
.B conn u
runs is.
.TP
\(bu
.B conn
reads the machine when something changes, not on a clock: Claude Code's
files, and what tmux says of its panes; the ports are read again one,
three and ten seconds after a pane changes, since a server listens a
while after it starts.
.TP
\(bu
The ground is fixed for the server's life unless a flag or the settings
change it.
.TP
\(bu
.B q
detaches.
The server and every process keep running.
.
